const e={note:"Heka is not 'magic' in the sense the word has carried since the Greeks. Egypt kept no register of an approved religion and a suspect art standing beside it: the same books, the same priests and the same House of Life supplied both. It is a creative force present at the first moment, older than the division of the world into pairs, and it is personified as a god who rides in the solar barque with Ra, beside Sia, perception, and Hu, authoritative utterance. It is what the gods possess, what the king exercises, what the physician applies and what the dead must carry through the Duat — the energy by which any effective act, royal, priestly, medical or funerary, actually takes effect. Our word 'magic' imports first a Greco-Roman suspicion of foreign rites and then a Christian polemic against them, and reads back into the Egyptian evidence a boundary the Egyptians did not draw in those terms. The qualification now usually entered is that this is a statement about categories and not about approval: Egyptian law and Egyptian ethics judged particular uses of heka with great severity, and a number of scholars continue to argue that 'magic' remains serviceable as an analytical term applied from outside, provided it is not mistaken for a native one. Egyptologists broadly treat heka as a neutral category of efficacious power, and these essays follow that usage. What stands below is description of what these texts say and what their practitioners did, and not instruction.",themes:[{slug:"heka-the-force",title:"Heka, the Force",native:"𓎛𓂓𓄿",translit:"ḥkꜣ",summary:"The force present before the world was divided: heka as cosmic energy, as a god in the sun's crew, and as the equipment issued to human beings for their defence.",paragraphs:["The Egyptian word ḥkꜣ names a force rather than a practice, and it is present at the first moment. In Coffin Texts spell 261 the speaker announces that he is Heka, made by the Lord of All before duality had come into being — before two things had yet separated out of the one, which is to say before there was a created order to work upon. Heka is therefore prior to the world and not a device within it, the energy by which anything whatever is brought about. This is why the word attaches with equal propriety to the sun's daily passage, to a royal decree, to a physician's poultice and to a mother's charm over a scorpion sting. Where a modern reader expects a category set apart from ordinary religion, Egyptian usage offers instead a category of efficacy: heka is what makes an act take effect.","Heka is also a god, written with the same signs and a seated divine determinative, and he receives cult; priests of Heka are attested from the Old Kingdom, and at Esna in the Ptolemaic and Roman periods he is the child of Khnum and his consort — Menhyt in one strand of the local theology, Nebtu in another — third member of the triad and a full participant in the temple's daily service. In the Book of Gates the sun's night barque is crewed by two figures flanking the god in his shrine, Sia at the prow and Heka at the stern: perception and efficacious power, the faculties by which the sun accomplishes its passage. Elsewhere Hu, the authoritative utterance, stands with them, so that the sun-god sees, speaks, and the speech takes effect. That Heka should be crew rather than cargo is the entire doctrine in a picture. He is figured as a man grasping two serpents, or as a child with a finger at his lip, the standard iconography of a divine child and a reminder that the force is understood to be newly born, at full strength, at every dawn.","Because it is a substance as much as a faculty, heka can be held in quantity, transferred and taken. The Cannibal Hymn of the Pyramid Texts, carved in the antechamber of the pyramid of Unas at Saqqara and repeated for Teti, has the dead king hunt the gods, cook them and eat them, so that their heka passes into his belly and their spirits into him; he becomes the greatest power in the sky because he has consumed the powers of those who held it. The Instruction for Merikare puts the same physics to a gentler end. The creator, that text says, made heka for mankind as a weapon, to ward off the blow of events — a defence issued to human beings against a world that would otherwise strike them without warning. Heka is thus not stolen from the gods in an Egyptian Prometheus story; it is provisioned, deliberately, as part of the equipment of being alive.","The technical literature works with a small and stable vocabulary. Heka is the power; ꜣḫw, akhu, are the effective utterances that deliver it — the word belongs to the same root as ꜣḫ, the transfigured and luminous state of a properly treated dead person, and to the sꜣḫw, the glorifications recited to produce that state. The third element is not a further kind of power but a technology: the mḏꜣt, the roll, in which a procedure was fixed with its materials, its gestures and its words, so that a successor could repeat it exactly, and which the rubrics repeatedly class as sštꜣ, secret or restricted. A practitioner required all three: the power, the words that carry it, and the book that governs the operation. The distinction is worth holding onto, because it explains the shape of the surviving evidence. What survives is overwhelmingly the manuals, from which the ꜣḫw can be read, and behind which the ḥkꜣ is everywhere assumed and nowhere argued for.","Heka is morally neutral in itself. The books that keep a child from a scorpion also fell an enemy; the lector who reads at a funeral could be, and sometimes was, employed against a rival. What Egyptian texts condemn is not heka but its use against the king, against the temple, or against a person unjustly — a question of law and of maat, not of category. There is no Egyptian term that stands to heka as 'magic' stands to 'religion'. That is a fact about vocabulary rather than about tolerance, and it should not be pressed into the claim that Egyptians drew no discriminations at all: they distinguished sharply between sanctioned and unsanctioned performance, and prosecuted the second as a capital matter. What they lacked was a name for a separate art. That name arrives with the Greeks, hardens in Roman legislation against foreign rites, and is sharpened again by Christian polemic, until Egypt is remembered as the homeland of an art it had never separated out."],terms:[{native:"𓎛𓂓𓄿",translit:"ḥkꜣ",gloss:"Heka: efficacious power, the energy by which an act takes effect; conventionally, and misleadingly, rendered 'magic'. V28-D28-G1."},{native:"𓎛𓂓𓄿𓀭",translit:"Ḥkꜣ",gloss:"The same word closed by the seated-god determinative A40: Heka as a deity, crew of the solar barque and divine child at Esna."},{native:"𓅜𓐍𓅱",translit:"ꜣḫw",gloss:"Akhu: effective utterances, the spoken instrument through which heka is applied. G25-Aa1-G43."},{native:"𓅜",translit:"ꜣḫ",gloss:"The transfigured, effective spirit of the dead; the root behind ꜣḫw and behind sꜣḫw, the recitations that produce that state."},{native:"mḏꜣt",translit:"mḏꜣt",gloss:"Given here in transliteration rather than hieroglyphs: the papyrus roll or book — the manual in which a procedure was fixed so that a successor could repeat it, and which the rubrics class as sštꜣ, restricted."},{native:"sı͗ꜣ",translit:"sı͗ꜣ",gloss:"In transliteration: Sia, perception or mind, personified at the prow of the sun's barque."},{native:"ḥw",translit:"ḥw",gloss:"In transliteration: Hu, the authoritative utterance that carries out what Sia has conceived."},{native:"ḥkꜣw",translit:"ḥkꜣw",gloss:"In transliteration: heka in the plural, 'powers'; used also of a person understood to command them."}],texts:[{title:"Coffin Texts, spell 261 (de Buck, The Egyptian Coffin Texts, volume III)",note:"Painted inside Middle Kingdom coffins from Middle Egypt: the speaker declares that he is Heka, fashioned by the Lord of All before duality had come into being, and warns the gods against obstructing him."},{title:"Pyramid Texts, utterances 273–274 (the 'Cannibal Hymn')",note:"Carved in the antechamber of the pyramid of Unas at Saqqara, c. 2350 BC, and repeated for Teti: the king snares, cooks and eats the gods so that their heka enters his belly."},{title:"The Instruction for Merikare (Papyrus Petersburg 1116A)",note:"Herakleopolitan or early Middle Kingdom teaching preserved in an Eighteenth Dynasty copy: the creator's provision of heka to mankind as a weapon against the blow of events."},{title:"The Book of Gates",note:"Royal netherworld book, from the tomb of Horemheb and the alabaster sarcophagus of Seti I onwards: the night barque is crewed by Sia and Heka alone, flanking the ram-headed sun in his shrine."},{title:"The temple of Esna, ritual and hymnic texts",note:"Ptolemaic and Roman inscriptions in which Heka is the divine child of Khnum and his consort and receives daily cult as a member of the local triad."}]},{slug:"the-lector-priest",title:"The Lector Priest",native:"ẖry-ḥbt",translit:"ẖry-ḥb(t), given in transliteration; 'kheri-hebet'",summary:"The professional who carried the roll: the lector priest, the House of Life that trained and supplied him, and the village practitioners who worked the same technique without the title.",paragraphs:["The professional at the centre of Egyptian ritual practice is the ẖry-ḥb(t), the lector priest — literally the man who is under, or who carries, the festival roll. The title describes his hands. In tomb and temple scenes he is the figure holding an open papyrus, often with a sash across his chest, reading aloud while others act: at the funeral he recites the offering list, at the Opening of the Mouth he prompts the sem priest through the sequence, in procession he speaks the god out of the sanctuary and back into it. The chief lector, ẖry-ḥbt ḥry-tp, was a senior temple officer, and in the second element of that title is commonly found the origin of the Hebrew ḥarṭummīm, the word Genesis and Exodus use for the magicians of Pharaoh's court. The derivation is the standard one, though it is not universally accepted; if it holds, the Bible preserves in that plural an Egyptian job description.","His competence was textual, and it was housed. The pr-ꜥnḫ, the House of Life, was a building attached to a temple in which the ritual books were composed, copied, corrected, stored and taught; it is attested at Abydos, at Amarna, at Edfu and Esna, and it survives in physical form in the Roman-period temple library of Tebtunis in the Fayum, whose papyri came out of the ground in quantity. Papyrus Salt 825 describes such a house at Abydos in theological terms, its four sides identified with gods and its contents guarded, and the manuals themselves carry rubrics insisting that a procedure has been proved and is not to be seen by any other eye. This is a scriptorium with a doctrine of secrecy, and the secrecy is technical rather than sinister: a miscopied spell does not work, and a book in the wrong hands works for the wrong person.","The lector stood among other offices. The ḥm-nṯr, god's servant or prophet, served the cult's higher functions; the wꜥb, the pure one, served by rotation in one of four phyles, a month in four, and passed his term in ritual cleanliness; the sem, in his panther skin, performed at the mouth of the mummy the acts that the lector read out. What distinguishes the lector is that he is the one who knows what the roll says. Outside the temple the same work was done without the title: village practitioners are called rḫ-ḫt, knower of things, and the feminine tꜣ rḫt, the wise woman, appears in the Deir el-Medina ostraca being consulted about why a child is ill and which god is angry, while the ḫrp Srqt, controller of Serqet, dealt with scorpion and snake. The line between temple and village runs through institutions and access to books, not through belief or method.","One burial shows the whole equipment together. Beneath the storerooms of the Ramesseum at Thebes, Quibell found in 1895–96 a shaft containing a wooden box that had held a working practitioner's library and apparatus: a group of late Middle Kingdom papyri — ritual and dramatic texts, hymns to Sobek, medical and magical prescriptions and, in the same box, the roll carrying the literary tales of Sinuhe and the Eloquent Peasant — together with a bronze wand in the form of a rearing cobra, fertility figurines, beads and amulets, and a wooden female figure wearing a lion mask. Nothing in that assemblage was felt to sit oddly beside anything else. Liturgy, literature, medicine and a serpent-shaped rod belonged to one man and to one competence, and the box is the best short answer to anyone who supposes Egyptian magic to have been a fringe activity.","The literary portrait of the profession is Papyrus Westcar. Khufu's sons entertain him with tales of the lectors of former reigns: Ubainer, whose wife takes a lover and who models a crocodile in wax, speaks over it, and has it thrown into the water, where it becomes a living beast of seven cubits, seizes the man, and returns to wax in the king's hand; Djadjaemankh, who folds one half of a lake back upon the other to recover a turquoise pendant from the bottom; and Djedi, a commoner of a hundred and ten, who eats prodigiously, reattaches the severed heads of a goose, a waterfowl and an ox, and knows the number of the secret chambers of the shrine of Thoth. Djedi refuses, pointedly, to perform the head-restoration upon a prisoner, since such a thing may not be done to the noble cattle of god. The tales are fiction, but the technique they describe is the technique of the manuals, and the wax figure was in earnest use: the trial records of the harem conspiracy against Ramesses III charge the defendants with precisely that."],terms:[{native:"ẖry-ḥbt",translit:"ẖry-ḥb(t)",gloss:"In transliteration rather than hieroglyphs: 'he who carries the festival roll', the lector priest, the reader at every rite."},{native:"ẖry-ḥbt ḥry-tp",translit:"ẖry-ḥbt ḥry-tp",gloss:"In transliteration: the chief lector priest; the ḥry-tp element is commonly, though not universally, taken to lie behind Hebrew ḥarṭummīm, 'the magicians of Egypt'."},{native:"𓉐𓋹",translit:"pr-ꜥnḫ",gloss:"The House of Life, O1 over S34: the temple scriptorium in which ritual, medical and magical books were copied, kept, guarded and taught."},{native:"𓊹𓍛",translit:"ḥm-nṯr",gloss:"'God's servant', the prophet; R8 written before U36 by honorific transposition, though read second."},{native:"wꜥb",translit:"wꜥb",gloss:"In transliteration: the 'pure one', the priest of the phyle rota whose qualification is ritual cleanliness."},{native:"sm",translit:"sm (stm)",gloss:"In transliteration: the sem priest in his panther skin, officiant at the Opening of the Mouth."},{native:"rḫ-ḫt",translit:"rḫ-ḫt",gloss:"In transliteration: 'knower of things'; with its feminine tꜣ rḫt, the wise woman, the village practitioner outside the temple rota."},{native:"ḫrp Srqt",translit:"ḫrp Srqt",gloss:"In transliteration: 'controller of Serqet', the scorpion goddess — the specialist in venom, attested from the Old Kingdom onwards."}],texts:[{title:"Papyrus Westcar (Papyrus Berlin 3033)",note:"Second Intermediate Period copy of tales generally assigned to the Middle Kingdom: Ubainer's wax crocodile, Djadjaemankh and the folded lake, and Djedi of Djed-Sneferu, who restores severed heads but refuses to work on a man."},{title:"The Ramesseum tomb group",note:"Shaft burial found by Quibell beneath the Ramesseum storerooms in 1895–96: a box of late Middle Kingdom papyri, now divided chiefly between the British Museum and Berlin, found with a bronze cobra wand, fertility figurines and a lion-masked figurine, now in Manchester."},{title:"Papyrus Salt 825 (BM EA 10051)",note:"Late Period ritual roll for the conservation of life, containing a theological description of the House of Life at Abydos, its sides identified with gods and its contents restricted."},{title:"The Book of the Temple",note:"A normative description of the ideal temple and its personnel, reconstructed by Quack from several dozen hieratic and Demotic fragments of the Roman period, listing the duties of the lector and of the House of Life."},{title:"The temple library of Tebtunis",note:"Roman-period papyri from the Fayum, now chiefly in Copenhagen and Florence: ritual, astronomical, medical and narrative rolls recovered from a functioning House of Life."},{title:"Deir el-Medina ostraca and the Chester Beatty papyri",note:"The written debris and private libraries of a Ramesside village of tomb-builders, recording consultations of the wise woman, dream manuals, and spells copied for household use."}]},{slug:"the-word-made-effective",title:"The Word Made Effective",native:"𓂋𓈖",translit:"rn",summary:"Speech creates and the name is a component of the person: the Memphite doctrine of heart and tongue, Isis extracting the secret name of Ra, and the erasure of names as a means of annihilation.",paragraphs:["The doctrine that speech creates is set out most fully on the Shabaka Stone, a slab of dark stone, usually described as black granite, in the British Museum, inscribed under the Kushite king Shabaka about 710 BC. Its opening announces that the king found a worm-eaten roll in the temple of Ptah at Memphis and had it copied afresh; in a later age the block was used as a millstone, and the grooves radiate from a central hole through the ruined middle of the text. Whether the composition is genuinely ancient is one of the sharper disputes in Egyptian philology. Breasted and Sethe read it as an Old Kingdom cosmogony of great antiquity; Junge and Schlögl argued in the 1970s that its language and theology belong to the Twenty-fifth Dynasty and its archaism is a deliberate manufacture; others place a core in the New Kingdom, while defenders of an early date continue to point to genuinely archaic grammar in parts of the text. What is not in dispute is the doctrine preserved. Ptah creates by mind and by mouth: he conceives the thing in the heart and commands it with the tongue; the Ennead of gods is his teeth and his lips; everything that is came into being through what the heart thought and the tongue ordered. Creation is a sentence correctly pronounced, and every subsequent recitation in Egyptian ritual is a small exercise of the same mechanism.","The person, in Egyptian anthropology, is an assembly: body, ka, ba, shadow and rn, the name. The name is not a label fixed from outside but a constituent, and it behaves like one. It must be spoken if the dead are to persist — hence the offering formula, which requires a passer-by to read it aloud, and Book of the Dead spell 25, provided so that a man may remember his own name in the god's domain. It can also be a passport. In spell 99 the deceased cannot cross the water until he has named every part of the ferry-boat in turn — the mooring-post, the mallet, the steering-oar, the wind — each of which answers only to its own name and refuses passage until it hears it. To know the name of a thing is to hold the handle by which it can be moved.","The classic demonstration is the narrative preserved in Papyrus Turin 1993. Isis, wanting the power that is Ra's alone, gathers the saliva falling from the mouth of the aged sun-god, kneads it with earth into a serpent, and leaves it on his path; the bite is not the bite of an ordinary snake, since the creature is made of the god himself. Ra burns, the gods can do nothing, and Isis offers a cure in exchange for his name. He recites a magnificent catalogue — Khepri in the morning, Ra at noon, Atum in the evening, maker of heaven, maker of the bull for the cow — and the venom does not abate, because none of these is the name. At last he consents, and the name passes out of his body into hers, on condition that it go no further than her son. The tale is not told for its own sake: it frames a remedy against poison, and the reciter over the patient speaks as Isis, one who now knows. The story exists so that its ending may be repeated over a sick body.","Because the name is a component, its destruction is a mode of killing. The erasure of Hatshepsut's names from her monuments, the systematic hacking out of Amun under Akhenaten and of Akhenaten's own name thereafter, the chiselling away of Seth in later periods, are not vandalism but operations. The same logic runs through the courts: in the Turin judicial papyrus recording the trial of the conspirators against Ramesses III the accused appear under substituted names — one is called Mesedsure, 'Ra hates him', another Pabekkamen, 'the blind servant' — so that the record itself continues the sentence for as long as anyone reads it. And in the sealed burial chambers of the earliest pyramids the danger is taken down to the level of the sign: hieroglyphs of snakes, lions and men are carved deliberately mutilated, cut in half or deprived of their legs, so that the words in which the king's own spells are written cannot rise up in the dark and act.","The script itself is called mdw-nṯr, the god's words. Every recitation in the ritual books is headed ḏd-mdw, 'words to be spoken', and it is the speaking, not the writing, that is operative — the writing being the guarantee that the speaking will be correct. When a spell was written on a strip of fine linen and laid at the throat, or set down in ink on a bowl, dissolved in liquid and drunk, these were not two different techniques but one text delivered into the same person by two routes. The doctrine is consistent from the Memphite Theology to the latest handbooks: what is properly conceived and correctly uttered comes to pass, and the whole apparatus of Egyptian practice exists to secure the correctness."],terms:[{native:"𓂋𓈖",translit:"rn",gloss:"The name, D21-N35: a constituent of the person alongside body, ka, ba and shadow, and the point at which the person can be reached."},{native:"ⲣⲁⲛ",translit:"ran",gloss:"The same word two thousand years later, in Coptic: ⲣⲁⲛ, 'name', the direct descendant of rn."},{native:"𓊪𓏏𓎛𓀭",translit:"Ptḥ",gloss:"Ptah of Memphis, Q3-X1-V28 with the seated-god determinative: he who conceives in the heart and commands with the tongue in the theology of the Shabaka Stone."},{native:"𓄣",translit:"ı͗b",gloss:"The heart, F34, as the organ of thought, intention and memory — the place where a thing is conceived before it is spoken."},{native:"𓄓",translit:"ns",gloss:"The tongue, F20: in the Memphite doctrine the instrument that issues what the heart has conceived, and so brings it into being."},{native:"𓊹𓌃",translit:"mdw-nṯr",gloss:"'The god's words': the Egyptian name for the hieroglyphic script itself; R8 stands first by honorific transposition though it is read second."},{native:"ḏd-mdw",translit:"ḏd-mdw",gloss:"In transliteration: 'words to be spoken', the rubric that heads a recitation and marks the point at which the text becomes an act."}],texts:[{title:"The Shabaka Stone (BM EA 498)",note:"Slab inscribed c. 710 BC, claiming to copy a worm-eaten roll from the temple of Ptah; the Memphite Theology of creation by heart and tongue. The date of the composition it preserves is actively disputed. Later reused as a millstone."},{title:"Papyrus Turin 1993, the Legend of Ra and Isis",note:"New Kingdom roll, with related material in Papyrus Chester Beatty XI: Isis makes a serpent of the sun-god's spittle and obtains his hidden name; the text serves as a remedy against poison."},{title:"Book of the Dead, spell 25",note:"'For causing a man to remember his name in the god's domain' — an explicit provision against the loss of the name after death."},{title:"Book of the Dead, spell 99, with its Coffin Text antecedents",note:"The ferryman spell, developed from the Middle Kingdom ferryman spells of the Coffin Texts: the deceased must name every component of the boat, and each part in turn refuses him until it hears its own name."},{title:"The Turin Judicial Papyrus (P. Turin 1875), with Papyrus Rollin and Papyrus Lee",note:"Records of the trial following the conspiracy against Ramesses III, in which the accused are entered under punitive substituted names such as Mesedsure, 'Ra hates him'."},{title:"The Pyramid Texts of Unas and Teti, Saqqara",note:"Spells carved with mutilated signs — snakes cut in two, birds without legs — so that the writing cannot animate within the sealed chamber."}]},{slug:"execration",title:"Execration",native:"sḫr",translit:"sḫr, given in transliteration; 'sekher', to fell, to overthrow",summary:"Naming an enemy, writing him on a pot or a figurine, and then breaking, burning and burying him: the best-documented ritual technology of the Middle Kingdom, and the daily temple defence of the sun against Apophis.",paragraphs:["The execration material consists of red pottery vessels and of small figurines of bound captives in clay and limestone — and, in the ritual texts if not in the surviving deposits, in wax — written over in hieratic with lists of enemies: rulers of Nubia, of Asia and of Libya, each named with his land and his mother's name; Egyptians, living and dead, who might turn; and then a closing clause of enormous breadth, covering every rebel who may rebel, every evil word, every evil plot, every evil dream, every evil slander. The Berlin sherds published by Sethe and the Brussels figurines published by Posener remain the core corpus, and they are, incidentally, among the earliest substantial sources for the rulers and place-names of Middle Bronze Age Palestine and Syria. The rite named its targets exhaustively and then covered whatever it might have missed, which is the characteristic Egyptian solution to the problem of an incomplete list.","What the inscribed objects do not record is what was done with them, and for that the decisive evidence is the deposit excavated by Jean Vercoutter at Mirgissa, the Egyptian fortress at the second cataract, in 1962–63. Outside the fort, in the desert, lay a pit containing the smashed remains of red pots inscribed with execration formulae, broken clay figurines of bound prisoners, some buried head downwards, and, a short distance away, a human skull placed face down with a flint blade beside it, in an arrangement most economically explained as the remains of a decapitation performed as part of the rite. Mirgissa supplies the choreography the texts omit: name, break, invert, bury, and do it at the desert margin, outside the ordered land.","The gestures were not improvised. Spitting, trampling, binding, sealing, reversing, breaking and burning belong to the same repertoire used in the temple's daily service, where the officiant treads upon the enemies of the god and the sacrificial animal is bound as Seth is bound. Robert Ritner's analysis of these acts made the point that mattered for the discipline: Egyptian ritual practice commands a single technical vocabulary of gesture, deployed in the sanctuary and in the desert pit alike, and there is no separable 'magical' method to be extracted from it. Colour and orientation carry meaning as the gestures do — red for Seth, for the desert and for danger; the left foot for treading; inversion for the annulment of a thing's proper order; the flint knife, an archaic instrument, for the cut.","The fullest surviving instructions belong to the ritual for the destruction of Apophis, preserved in the Bremner-Rhind papyrus, a Theban roll whose colophon places it in the reign of Alexander IV, about 310 BC, now in the British Museum. Apophis is the serpent of dissolution who attacks the sun's barque nightly, and his overthrow was not an emergency measure but a standing duty of the temple, performed at the hours of the day and night and at the great festivals. The rubrics were exact. A figure of the serpent was to be drawn in green on a fresh sheet of papyrus, or modelled in wax, with his name written upon him; he was then wrapped and bound, spat upon, trodden underfoot with the left foot, cut with a flint knife and burnt, and his ashes destroyed. The book also catalogues his names, so that each might be recited and annulled in turn, and it closes on the flat declaration that he shall not exist. Cosmic maintenance and the destruction of a personal enemy proceeded by one method.","The same technique operated at every scale. Figures of bound foreigners were set beneath thresholds and pavements so that the king trod on them as he walked; the nine bows, the conventional set of Egypt's enemies, appear on the soles of royal sandals and on the footstool of Tutankhamun's throne, where the king's feet rested upon them as he sat. Red pots were smashed at the close of funerals from the Old Kingdom onwards, and their sherds lie at tomb entrances. And the technique could be turned against the palace: the documents of the conspiracy against Ramesses III record the use of spells taken from the royal library, and of wax figures made to disable the guards — an accusation the court treated as a capital matter of fact, and prosecuted as such."],terms:[{native:"ḫftı͗w",translit:"ḫftı͗w",gloss:"In transliteration rather than hieroglyphs: 'enemies', the standing category upon which the rite operates, foreign and domestic alike."},{native:"sbı͗",translit:"sbı͗",gloss:"In transliteration: the rebel, one who rises against the established order; Apophis is the rebel in his cosmic form."},{native:"ꜥꜣpp",translit:"ꜥꜣpp",gloss:"In transliteration: Apophis, the serpent of dissolution who assaults the solar barque at every hour of the night."},{native:"ı͗sft",translit:"ı͗sft",gloss:"In transliteration: disorder, injustice, falsehood — the standing opposite of maat, and what the enemy embodies."},{native:"mꜣꜥt",translit:"mꜣꜥt",gloss:"In transliteration: right order, truth and justice, the condition the rite is performed to maintain."},{native:"𓂧𓈙𓂋",translit:"dšr",gloss:"'Red', spelt here in three uniliteral signs, D46-N37-D21: the colour of Seth, of the desert and of the pots broken in the rite."},{native:"sḏ dšrwt",translit:"sḏ dšrwt",gloss:"In transliteration: 'the breaking of the red pots', the smashing that concluded funerary and execration rituals alike."}],texts:[{title:"The Berlin execration texts (Sethe, Die Ächtung feindlicher Fürsten, Völker und Dinge)",note:"Middle Kingdom hieratic written on red pottery, later smashed: lists of Nubian, Asiatic and Libyan rulers with their mothers' names, Egyptian potential rebels, and a closing clause against every evil word, plot and dream."},{title:"The Brussels execration figurines (Posener, Princes et pays d'Asie et de Nubie)",note:"Bound-captive figures in clay and limestone, said to come from the Saqqara region, carrying a longer and later recension of the same lists."},{title:"The Mirgissa deposit",note:"Excavated by Vercoutter at the second-cataract fortress in 1962–63: a desert pit with smashed inscribed pots, inverted figurines of prisoners, and, nearby, a face-down human skull with a flint blade."},{title:"Papyrus Bremner-Rhind (BM EA 10188), the Book of Overthrowing Apophis",note:"Theban roll of c. 310 BC: the daily temple ritual against the serpent, with rubrics for drawing him in green on new papyrus, naming, wrapping, spitting, trampling and burning, and a catalogue of his names to be annulled."},{title:"The Turin Judicial Papyrus (P. Turin 1875), with Papyrus Rollin and Papyrus Lee",note:"Trial records of the harem conspiracy against Ramesses III, charging the use of writings from the royal library and of wax figures made to incapacitate the palace guard."}]},{slug:"amulets-and-the-protected-body",title:"Amulets and the Protected Body",native:"𓎃",translit:"sꜣ",summary:"Signs made three-dimensional and worn: the operative logic of form, material and consecration, from the household ivory wand to the heart scarab that must keep silent at the weighing.",paragraphs:["An Egyptian amulet is not a token of belief but a device, and three properties make it work. Its form is a word: the ꜥnḫ is 'life', the ḏd 'stability', the sꜣ 'protection', the wḏꜣt 'soundness' — each amulet is a hieroglyph made three-dimensional and hung on the body, so that the wearer carries the sign as a sentence carries it. Its material is operative in its own right: green stone for renewal and vegetation, red carnelian and jasper for blood, gold for the incorruptible flesh of the gods, lapis for the sky and for the hair of the divine. And it is spoken over: the amulet chapters of the Book of the Dead specify not only the object and its stone but the words to be said above it, and sometimes the day on which it was to be made.","The wedjat eye is the eye of Horus, torn out in the struggle with Seth and made whole again by Thoth, and the word wḏꜣ itself means to be sound, uninjured, prosperous; the amulet is a completed healing worn as an object, which is why it was laid over the embalmer's incision as readily as at the throat. The ḏd pillar is the backbone of Osiris, and the raising of it at Memphis was a royal festival — worn at the neck it holds the wearer upright. The tı͗t, the Knot of Isis, is prescribed by Book of the Dead spell 156 in red jasper, and the words spoken over it invoke the blood of Isis, her power and her magic as protection. The scarab is the verb ḫpr, to come into being: the beetle rolling its ball was understood as the shape in which the sun makes itself each morning. The sꜣ, conventionally explained as a rolled reed shelter or a herdsman's life-preserver, is what Bes and Taweret carry in their hands at the bedside.","The heart scarab is the most legally minded object in Egyptian religion. At the weighing, the heart is at once the man's organ of conscience and the witness against him, and Book of the Dead spell 30B, inscribed on the flat underside of a large scarab, addresses it directly: the heart of his mother is not to stand up against him as a witness, not to oppose him before the keeper of the balance, not to make his name stink to the officials of the court. The rubric prescribes a scarab of green stone, mounted in gold and set at the throat of the dead. Spell 125 supplies the declaration of innocence before the forty-two judges; spell 30B supplies the guarantee that the man's own body will not contradict it. Together they constitute a complete procedural strategy.","On the corpse the amulets form a system, placed by rule. The Ritual of Embalming, known from Papyrus Boulaq 3 in Cairo and Papyrus Louvre 5158, sets out the bandaging in sequence with the oils and the amulets belonging to each stage and each limb, and the words recited over them as they were laid. Book of the Dead spells 155 to 160 make up a small amulet series — the ḏd of gold, the knot of red jasper, the vulture collar, the papyrus column of green feldspar — each with its material and its rubric. When Carter unwrapped Tutankhamun, a hundred and forty-three objects lay among the wrappings, disposed in ordered layers between the linen. The mummy is, among other things, a bound assemblage of activated signs.","The living were as thoroughly equipped. Middle Kingdom households owned curved plaques of hippopotamus ivory, conventionally and misleadingly called wands, carved with processions of protective beings — the lion-dwarf, the frog, the knife-bearing griffin, the turtle — and inscribed with words such as the cutting off of the head of any enemy who enters the chamber of the child; several are worn at one end, as though drawn along the ground to mark out a circle, and they are found in the graves of women and of children. Bes and Taweret amulets, the painted birth bricks of which one survives from a Middle Kingdom house at South Abydos, knotted cords with a bead at each knot, and later the small stelae of Horus upon the crocodiles set beside the door, belong to the same domestic register. Protection was continuous, material and worn on the person."],terms:[{native:"𓎃",translit:"sꜣ",gloss:"The sa, V17: the sign and amulet of protection, conventionally explained as a rolled reed shelter, though the object behind the sign is not certain; carried by Bes and Taweret."},{native:"𓂀",translit:"wḏꜣt",gloss:"The wedjat, D10, the eye of Horus restored; the root wḏꜣ means to be sound and whole, so the amulet is a healing already accomplished."},{native:"𓊽",translit:"ḏd",gloss:"The djed pillar, R11, understood as the backbone of Osiris and as endurance; prescribed in gold by Book of the Dead spell 155."},{native:"𓎬",translit:"tı͗t",gloss:"The tyet or Knot of Isis, V39; spell 156 prescribes it in red jasper and invokes the blood and power of the goddess over it."},{native:"𓋹",translit:"ꜥnḫ",gloss:"Life, S34: the sign held to the nostrils of the king by the gods, and worn as the commonest of all amulet forms."},{native:"𓆣",translit:"ḫprr",gloss:"The scarab beetle, L1, written for the verb ḫpr, 'to come into being'; the sun's self-creation each morning."},{native:"𓇅",translit:"wꜣḏ",gloss:"Fresh, green, flourishing, M13; also the papyrus-column amulet prescribed in green feldspar by spells 159 and 160."},{native:"ḥrst",translit:"ḥrst",gloss:"In transliteration: carnelian, the red stone chosen for amulets that operate upon blood."}],texts:[{title:"Book of the Dead, spell 30B",note:"The heart scarab text, addressed to the heart itself before the balance; the rubric prescribes green stone mounted in gold and placed at the throat."},{title:"Book of the Dead, spells 155–160",note:"The amulet series, each spell naming its object and its material: the djed of gold, the knot of red jasper, the collars, the papyrus column of green feldspar."},{title:"The Ritual of Embalming (Papyrus Boulaq 3, Cairo CG 58027; Papyrus Louvre 5158)",note:"Roman-period manuals giving the order of wrappings, unguents and amulets limb by limb, with the recitation belonging to each."},{title:"Middle Kingdom apotropaic wands",note:"Curved hippopotamus-ivory plaques carved with protective beings and inscribed for the safety of mother and child; found in the burials of women and children, several worn at the tip."},{title:"The wrappings of Tutankhamun (Carter's excavation record)",note:"A hundred and forty-three amulets and items of jewellery recorded in ordered layers within the bandages, the fullest surviving instance of an amulet programme in place."}]},{slug:"medicine-and-magic",title:"Medicine and Magic Together",native:"swnw",translit:"swnw, given in transliteration; 'sunu', the physician",summary:"One roll gives a surgical protocol and an incantation without apology: the medical papyri, the logic of mythological precedent, and the cippi over which water was poured and drunk.",paragraphs:["The medical papyri are the largest technical literature surviving from Egypt: the Ebers papyrus at Leipzig, some hundred and ten columns and over eight hundred prescriptions; the Edwin Smith papyrus in New York, forty-eight surgical cases running from the head downwards; the Hearst papyrus from Deir el-Ballas; the Berlin, London, Chester Beatty and Kahun rolls; the medical papyri from the Ramesseum box. Most are New Kingdom copies of Middle Kingdom compositions, several visibly assembled from older sources with the joins still showing. The point that matters for the history of ideas is structural. A single roll gives a surgical procedure and an incantation in consecutive entries, with no apology, no marker of a change of register and no sign that the compiler thought he had crossed a line.","The Edwin Smith papyrus shows how disciplined the observational side could be. Each case follows a fixed protocol — title, examination, diagnosis, verdict, treatment — with the examination framed in the second person, 'if you examine a man having such a wound', and the verdict drawn from three standing formulae: an ailment I will treat; an ailment with which I will contend; an ailment not to be treated. The third is a decision to withhold intervention, recorded as a professional judgement rather than a failure. The text contains the earliest known written description of the brain and its coverings, observes that a head wound may disable the limbs, and breaks off mid-sentence at the spine. Its incantations are on the back of the same roll, where a spell against the pestilence of the year sits beside recipes for the skin. Surgery and conjuration are the two faces of one document.","The spells are not ornament upon a rational practice; they have a mechanism, and the mechanism is precedent. A magico-medical spell characteristically recounts a short myth in which a god suffered exactly what the patient suffers and was cured, and then asserts the identity of the two cases: as was done for Horus, so it is done for this sufferer, born of this mother. The practitioner speaks in the first person as Isis, or Thoth, or Horus himself, and the patient is not analogous to the god but, for the duration of the recitation, the same. This is why the mother's name is given and not the father's, identification having to be exact, and why the rubrics insist so often that a remedy is true, that it has been proved, that it is effective a million times. Precedent is the evidence, and the archive of what has worked is the pharmacopoeia.","The precedent invoked most often is the poisoning of the child Horus in the marshes of Chemmis, where Isis has hidden him from Seth. She leaves him; he is stung; she returns to find him rigid with foam at his lips, and cries out until Thoth comes — and the sun's barque halts in the sky and will not proceed until the child is healed. The narrative is a piece of ritual engineering. It makes every poisoned child that child, and it places the entire cosmos under obligation, since the sun cannot move while the case remains open. The Metternich Stela, cut in greywacke under Nectanebo II in the fourth century BC, carries this story with a dozen related spells, among them the tale of Isis and the seven scorpions who accompanied her in her flight.","That stela is the grandest example of a common object. The cippus is a small plaque showing the naked child Horus standing upon crocodiles and gripping serpents, scorpions, a lion and an oryx in his fists, the mask of Bes above his head and every remaining surface covered with the spells against venom. It was used hydraulically. Water was poured over it so that it ran across the inscribed text, was caught in a basin at the base, and was then drunk by the patient or poured over his body. The healing statues worked in the same manner: the priest Djedhor of Athribis had himself carved as a squatting figure covered in spells and seated in a basin, a public utility for the bitten of his town. The words were administered as a medicine, and the practice continued into the Roman period without alteration of principle."],terms:[{native:"swnw",translit:"swnw",gloss:"In transliteration rather than hieroglyphs: the physician, a titled profession with its own hierarchy, including a chief physician of the king."},{native:"pḫrt",translit:"pḫrt",gloss:"In transliteration: a remedy or prescription, the unit of the medical papyri, given with ingredients, quantities and mode of application."},{native:"𓄣",translit:"ı͗b",gloss:"The heart, F34: in the Ebers 'Book of the Heart' the organ from which the vessels of the body run, and which speaks in every limb."},{native:"ḥꜣty",translit:"ḥꜣty",gloss:"In transliteration: the second word for heart, conventionally taken as the physical organ where ı͗b tends to the seat of thought and will, though the two overlap in use."},{native:"mtw",translit:"mtw",gloss:"In transliteration: the vessels or ducts running from the heart, carrying blood, air, water and, in disease, morbid matter."},{native:"šnı͗",translit:"šnı͗",gloss:"In transliteration: to conjure, to exorcise — the verb used of speaking over a patient or a poison."},{native:"ḫrp Srqt",translit:"ḫrp Srqt",gloss:"In transliteration: 'controller of Serqet', the specialist in scorpion and snake, attached to expeditions and to the court."},{native:"Ḥrw-šd",translit:"Ḥrw-šd",gloss:"In transliteration: Horus the Saviour, the child shown on the cippi treading crocodiles and gripping venomous creatures."}],texts:[{title:"The Ebers Papyrus (Leipzig University Library)",note:"c. 1550 BC, some hundred and ten columns and over eight hundred prescriptions, opening with spells to be recited when any remedy is applied, and including the 'Book of the Heart' on the vessels of the body."},{title:"The Edwin Smith Papyrus (New York Academy of Medicine)",note:"Forty-eight surgical cases in a fixed protocol of examination, diagnosis, verdict and treatment, breaking off mid-sentence at the spine; incantations against the pestilence of the year on the verso of the same roll."},{title:"The Hearst Papyrus (Bancroft Library, Berkeley)",note:"From Deir el-Ballas: a working household or practitioner's collection of remedies, overlapping in places word for word with Ebers."},{title:"The London Medical Papyrus (BM EA 10059)",note:"Sixty-odd entries, the majority of them incantations, including spells recorded as being in the language of Keftiu — among the earliest notices of a foreign ritual tongue in Egyptian medicine."},{title:"Papyrus Leiden I 343 + I 345",note:"A Ramesside magico-medical roll directed against disease-demons and the agents of illness, preserving spells with their mythological precedents intact."},{title:"The Metternich Stela (Metropolitan Museum of Art 50.85) and the healing statue of Djedhor (Cairo JE 46341)",note:"The fullest cippus, made under Nectanebo II, with the spells of Isis and the seven scorpions; and the Athribis statue seated in its own basin, over which water was poured and collected for the patient."}]},{slug:"the-books-of-the-dead",title:"The Books of the Dead",native:"prt m hrw",translit:"prt m hrw, given in transliteration; 'peret em heru', going forth by day",summary:"One tradition in three phases: the Pyramid Texts in a sealed royal chamber, the Coffin Texts on provincial coffin walls, and the papyrus rolls of going forth by day, with their judgement, their answerers and their charts of the night.",paragraphs:["The three great funerary corpora are one tradition in three phases, and it is a mistake to read them as three books. The Pyramid Texts are the oldest: columns of vertical hieroglyphs carved in the burial chamber and antechamber of the pyramid of Unas at Saqqara at the end of the Fifth Dynasty, about 2350 BC, and thereafter in the pyramids of kings and of queens for some two centuries. Sethe's edition numbered 759 utterances; the later clearance of the queens' pyramids at South Saqqara has carried the known total well beyond that. They are spells for a body that must get up and go out — ascent texts, offering liturgies, protective spells against snakes — and their arrangement follows the route outwards from the sarcophagus chamber. In those sealed rooms the signs of dangerous creatures are carved mutilated, cut in half or left without legs, so that the writing cannot come alive around the king.","In the Middle Kingdom the same kind of material is painted on the interiors of wooden coffins belonging to nomarchs and officials at Deir el-Bersha, Asyut and Meir; de Buck's edition prints 1,185 spells, many descended directly from Pyramid Text utterances and many entirely new. On the floors of certain Bersha coffins is the Book of Two Ways, the earliest illustrated map of the beyond: two winding routes, one of water and one of land, drawn in blue and in black, running side by side through gates with named keepers, a lake of fire lying between them. The old textbook account of all this as the 'democratisation of the afterlife' is now heavily qualified, and by some scholars — Harold Hays most flatly — rejected outright. What changed may be as much the wealth and independence of provincial officials, and the accident that wooden coffins survive, as any doctrinal extension of a formerly royal privilege.","From the early Eighteenth Dynasty the corpus moves onto papyrus, and the Egyptians call it prt m hrw, going forth by day — the object of the book being the ability to leave the tomb and return to it. There is no fixed edition. Copies were compiled to order, some magnificent and some produced in workshops with blank spaces left for the owner's name, and the vignettes matter as much as the text. The numbering in use today carries no ancient authority: it descends from Lepsius's publication in 1842 of a Ptolemaic roll at Turin, and modern counts reach something like a hundred and ninety-two spells. The great copies — the papyrus of Nu, the papyrus of Ani, the papyrus of Hunefer — differ from one another in selection, order and length, and no two rolls are the same book.","Spell 125 is the judgement. The dead man enters the Hall of the Two Truths, addresses Osiris, and then turns to the forty-two assessors, each with his name and his town — the fire-embracer, the bone-breaker, the one who came from Heliopolis — denying to each a particular offence: I have not done wrong, I have not stolen, I have not caused pain, I have not held back water in its season, I have not diminished the grain measure. The form is a negative declaration addressed by name to each judge in turn, while the heart is weighed against the feather of maat, Thoth records the result and the composite devourer waits beside the scales. It is a hearing conducted by exact address, and the amulet at the throat has already been instructed to keep the heart quiet.","Spell 6 solves a different problem. The fields of the beyond require labour — shifting sand from east to west, filling the channels, carrying — and the dead are liable to conscription for it. The spell activates a small mummiform figure, the shabti, so that when the summons comes the figure steps forward and answers in its owner's place: here I am, it says. What begins as a single substitute becomes, by the Late Period, a workforce of three hundred and sixty-five with thirty-six overseers, and Tutankhamun was buried with more than four hundred. The kings meanwhile had a literature of their own on the tomb walls — the Amduat, complete in the tomb of Thutmose III, the Book of Gates about the sarcophagus of Seti I, the Book of Caverns, the Litany of Ra — which are not spells for the dead so much as hour-by-hour charts of the sun's passage through the night, every being in them named and captioned. To know the plan and the names is itself the qualification."],terms:[{native:"prt m hrw",translit:"prt m hrw",gloss:"In transliteration rather than hieroglyphs: 'going forth by day', the Egyptian title of what modern scholarship calls the Book of the Dead."},{native:"𓂋",translit:"rꜣ",gloss:"The mouth, D21, and so an utterance or spell: each chapter of the funerary books is a rꜣ, a thing said."},{native:"𓂓",translit:"kꜣ",gloss:"The ka, D28, the vital double sustained by offerings, for which the tomb chapel and its food supply exist."},{native:"𓅽",translit:"bꜣ",gloss:"The ba, G53, shown as a human-headed bird: the mobile aspect of a person, which leaves the tomb by day and returns at night."},{native:"𓇽",translit:"dwꜣt",gloss:"The Duat, N15, the region traversed by the sun and by the dead between sunset and sunrise."},{native:"𓐙𓊤",translit:"mꜣꜥ-ḫrw",gloss:"'True of voice', Aa11 with P8, the oar: vindicated — the status conferred by the judgement and appended to the name of the dead thereafter."},{native:"šꜣbtı͗",translit:"šꜣbtı͗ / wšbtı͗",gloss:"In transliteration: the shabti or ushabti, the figure that answers the summons to labour on its owner's behalf."},{native:"sꜣḫw",translit:"sꜣḫw",gloss:"In transliteration: the 'glorifications', recitations that make the dead an ꜣḫ — the funerary application of the akhu of the first essay."}],texts:[{title:"The Pyramid Texts, pyramid of Unas, Saqqara",note:"c. 2350 BC: the earliest corpus, carved in the burial chamber and antechamber, with dangerous signs deliberately mutilated; 759 utterances in Sethe's edition, with more recovered since from the queens' pyramids."},{title:"The Coffin Texts (de Buck, 1,185 spells)",note:"Middle Kingdom spells painted inside wooden coffins from Deir el-Bersha, Asyut and Meir, many descended from Pyramid Text utterances and many new."},{title:"The Book of Two Ways",note:"Painted on the floorboards of coffins from Deir el-Bersha: the earliest illustrated map of the hereafter, with a water route and a land route in blue and black, gates, keepers and a lake of fire."},{title:"The Papyrus of Nu (BM EA 10477)",note:"One of the earliest substantial Eighteenth Dynasty rolls of going forth by day, and a principal witness for the older wording of many spells."},{title:"The Papyrus of Ani (BM EA 10470) and the Papyrus of Hunefer (BM EA 9901)",note:"Nineteenth Dynasty Theban rolls carrying the fullest painted versions of spell 125 and of the weighing of the heart."},{title:"The Amduat and the Book of Gates",note:"Royal netherworld books: the Amduat complete in the tomb of Thutmose III (KV34), the Book of Gates in the tomb of Horemheb and on the alabaster sarcophagus of Seti I in Sir John Soane's Museum — hour-by-hour charts of the sun's night with every being named."}]},{slug:"the-late-papyri",title:"The Late Papyri",native:"ϩⲓⲕ",translit:"hik — Coptic ϩⲓⲕ, 'magic', from ḥkꜣ",summary:"The bilingual handbooks of Roman Thebes, the Greek papyri that stand beside them, and the process by which a temple functionary became, for outsiders, the archetype of the magician.",paragraphs:["In the 1820s the collector Giovanni Anastasi, Swedish-Norwegian consul in Egypt, acquired at Thebes a group of papyri long reconstructed as a single ancient holding: a working library of ritual handbooks in Greek and Demotic, of the third and fourth centuries AD, now divided between Paris, London and Leiden. That the rolls came from one find is an inference from their dealer history and their contents rather than an excavated fact, and it has lately been questioned; but the group is coherent, and it remains the single richest source for late Egyptian practice. Its Demotic centrepiece, the Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, survives in two halves, one in the British Museum and one at Leiden, and reads like a professional's working file: procedures for lamp divination and for vessel divination, for sending dreams, for binding a lover, for curing scorpion sting and dog bite, and a decoded list of plant names in which the temple's ingredient-cyphers are spelled out for the user.","The continuity with temple practice is direct rather than nostalgic. The pḥ-nṯr, the 'arrival of the god', is an inquiry in which the operator brings the god down into a lamp flame or into a bowl of oil and water while a boy medium reports what he sees — a technique with a long Egyptian pedigree and priestly personnel. The recitations are written in Demotic with glosses in Old Coptic: Egyptian set down in Greek letters with additional signs, and with the vowels written, which no Egyptian script had ever troubled to do. That orthographic invention was made because the sound of a name had to be reproduced exactly. It is the doctrine of the Isis story, three thousand years on, at last equipped with a phonetic notation.","The Greek papyri of the same library, collected in modern editions as the Papyri Graecae Magicae, hold the same technology in another language. The great Paris codex, PGM IV, runs to thousands of lines and includes the ascent text that Albrecht Dieterich named the Mithras Liturgy — a title of his making, and its Mithraic character has been disputed ever since — with bowl and lamp divinations and invocations addressing Egyptian gods under Greek names and Jewish ones under both; the Leiden rolls PGM XII and XIII include the compilation called the Eighth Book of Moses. Strings of vowels, palindromes and untranslatable voces magicae fill the invocations. These are not corruptions of Egyptian practice but its late idiom: a name that cannot be translated is a name that must be pronounced, and the pronunciation was always the point.","Meanwhile Egypt had become, for its neighbours, the homeland of magic. The lector priest walks into the Hebrew Bible as the ḥarṭōm; he walks into Demotic literature as Setne Khamwas, son of Ramesses II and a historical high priest of Ptah, who in the Cairo papyrus robs a tomb for the Book of Thoth — whose two spells are said to let a reader enchant sky and earth and understand what the birds and the creeping things say — and pays for it with his family; and he walks into Greek fiction as Pancrates of Memphis in Lucian's Lover of Lies, a sacred scribe who had spent twenty-three years in underground sanctuaries and who animates a pestle to fetch water, the tale that became the sorcerer's apprentice. Behind each portrait stands a temple functionary with a book.","The institutions closed slowly. The last dated hieroglyphic inscription was cut at Philae in 394 and the last Demotic graffito there in 452, and the temple economies that had trained and paid the scribes went with them. The technique did not stop. Coptic handbooks of the following centuries preserve the same structures — the little precedent-myth, the exact naming, the prescribed materials, the written charm dissolved and drunk — with archangels and the cross standing where the older names had stood. And the word itself survived into Christian Egyptian: Coptic ϩⲓⲕ, hik, 'magic', is ḥkꜣ, some three thousand years after the coffin on which a man announced that he was Heka, born before duality had come to be."],terms:[{native:"ϩⲓⲕ",translit:"hik",gloss:"Coptic ϩⲓⲕ, 'magic' — the direct descendant of ḥkꜣ, still current in the Christian language of Egypt."},{native:"𓅝𓏏𓏭",translit:"Ḏḥwty",gloss:"Thoth, G26-X1-Z4, patron of the scribe and of the House of Life, whose books the late tales make the object of every magician's ambition."},{native:"pḥ-nṯr",translit:"pḥ-nṯr",gloss:"In transliteration: 'the arrival of the god', the Demotic term for lamp and vessel divination performed through a boy medium."},{native:"sš pr-ꜥnḫ",translit:"sš pr-ꜥnḫ",gloss:"In transliteration: 'scribe of the House of Life', the professional title that Greek writers render as the Egyptian magician."},{native:"Stne",translit:"Stne (Setne) Ḫꜥ-m-wꜣst",gloss:"In transliteration: Setne Khamwas, historical high priest of Ptah and son of Ramesses II, who becomes the hero of the Demotic magician tales; 'Setne' derives from his priestly title sm."},{native:"μαγεία",translit:"mageia",gloss:"The Greek word, originally naming Persian priestly craft, under which Egyptian temple technique was reclassified — and by which it was eventually condemned."}],texts:[{title:"The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden (BM EA 10070 + Leiden I 383)",note:"Third century AD, from Thebes: lamp and vessel divination with a boy medium, dream-sending, bindings, cures for bite and sting, and a decoded list of coded plant names; Demotic with Old Coptic glosses that write the vowels."},{title:"PGM IV, the Great Magical Papyrus of Paris (Bibliothèque nationale, suppl. gr. 574)",note:"The largest Greek handbook of the group, containing the text Dieterich named the Mithras Liturgy, bowl and lamp divinations, bindings and long invocations mixing Egyptian, Greek and Jewish divine names."},{title:"PGM XII and XIII (Leiden I 384 and I 395)",note:"Companion rolls from the same library, including the compilation known as the Eighth Book of Moses, with a Demotic section on one roll."},{title:"Setne Khamwas I (Papyrus Cairo 30646) and Setne Khamwas II (Papyrus BM EA 604)",note:"Ptolemaic and Roman Demotic tales: the theft of the Book of Thoth from the tomb of Naneferkaptah, and the journey of Si-Osire through the netherworld."},{title:"The Book of Thoth (Demotic, ed. Jasnow and Zauzich)",note:"A dialogue between a disciple and Thoth on scribal and priestly knowledge, reconstructed from many Ptolemaic and Roman fragments: the milieu from which the late handbooks came."},{title:"Lucian, Philopseudes 34–36",note:"Second-century satire preserving the figure of Pancrates of Memphis, sacred scribe, twenty-three years underground in the sanctuaries — the earliest form of the sorcerer's apprentice."}]},{slug:"dreams-and-oracles",title:"Dreams and Oracles",native:"rswt",translit:"rswt, given in transliteration; 'resut', a dream",summary:"Sleep as a place of contact and the god's image as a source of judgements: the Ramesside dream manual, incubation in the sanatoria, the barque oracle at Deir el-Medina and the decrees worn at the neck.",paragraphs:["A dream in Egyptian is rswt, a word formed on the root rs, to wake, and the state it names is one of contact rather than of imagination: in sleep a person is somewhere the gods and the dead can reach. The Ramesside dream manual on the recto of Papyrus Chester Beatty III, from the Deir el-Medina library of the scribe Qenherkhepeshef, is a professional's reference table. Its entries are grouped by the type of the dreamer — one section for the man who is a follower of Horus, a second and badly damaged one for the follower of Seth — and run in columns: if a man sees himself in a dream doing such a thing, then 'good', or, written in red, 'bad', and then the meaning. The reasoning is frequently phonetic, the dreamed object read through a word that sounds like its name, which is exactly how a scribe trained on hieroglyphic punning would read a sign. The roll closes with a remedy for a man troubled by nightmare, recited over herbs and bread and beer, in which the dreamer speaks as Horus and Isis comes to him.","Deliberate dreaming — incubation, sleeping within a sacred precinct to obtain an answer — is best attested late, though its logic is old. Ptolemaic and Roman visitors slept in the sanatorium at Dendera, in the chambers at Deir el-Bahri where Imhotep and Amenhotep son of Hapu had become gods of healing and where Greek pilgrims scratched their thanks into the walls, and in the painted Bes chambers at Saqqara, whose function is disputed but which are usually read as rooms for incubation. At the Memphite Serapeum in the second century BC a Greek recluse, Ptolemaios son of Glaukias, wrote his dreams down as they came; from the same site the Demotic ostraca of Hor of Sebennytos record his dreams and the reports he made of them to the authorities. Royal use is older and franker: on the stela set between the paws of the Great Sphinx, Thutmose IV relates that the god spoke to him as he slept in the shadow of the head and promised him the throne if he would clear away the sand.","The oracle made the god answer in public. At Deir el-Medina the deified Amenhotep I was carried out on festival days in his portable barque on the shoulders of the workmen, and questions were put to him: two ostraca, one bearing each alternative, might be set before him, or the matter spoken aloud, and the barque moved forward for assent and drew back for refusal. What survives is the paperwork — ostraca recording who took a tool, whose the disputed tomb-plot was, whether a man should accept an office. The god adjudicated property and theft, and his verdict was entered into the record and could be appealed to a second god. The mechanism is entirely unembarrassed about the human bearers: the god acts through the men who carry him, as heka acts through the hands that perform it.","In the Third Intermediate Period the oracle's speech was miniaturised into an object. The oracular amuletic decrees are long, narrow rolls of hieratic, worn in cylindrical cases at the neck, in which a god — most often Amun or Khonsu — speaks in the first person and undertakes to keep a named child safe, itemising the dangers at exhausting length: every illness and every demon of the year, the bite of every snake and every scorpion, the collapse of a wall, blindness, the anger of gods and of the dead, the evil eye, and the magic of a Syrian or a Nubian. They are contracts, and they read like contracts. Close to them in spirit are the ear stelae of Memphis and Deir el-Medina, small slabs carved with pairs of ears, sometimes dozens of them, dedicated to gods called hearers of petitions — the ear being the point at which a correctly framed utterance enters the god, as the mouth is the point at which it leaves the man.","What binds this material to everything above it is institutional as much as doctrinal. The dream manuals were House of Life property, copied by the same scribes who copied the medical and ritual rolls; the amuletic decrees were written by temple personnel; the oracle was administered by priests who carried the barque. The whole apparatus rests on one conviction, held without embarrassment from the Pyramid Texts to the Coptic handbooks: that a properly conceived and correctly delivered utterance obliges a response, whether from a poison, an enemy, a heart upon the scales, or a god in his shrine."],terms:[{native:"rswt",translit:"rswt",gloss:"In transliteration rather than hieroglyphs: a dream, formed on the root rs, 'to wake' — the dreamer is understood as awake elsewhere."},{native:"wḏ",translit:"wḏ",gloss:"In transliteration: a decree or command; the form taken by the god's answer in the oracular amuletic decrees."},{native:"sḏm nḥwt",translit:"sḏm nḥwt",gloss:"In transliteration: 'who hears petitions', the epithet of gods addressed at the ear stelae and at the outer walls of temples."},{native:"msḏr",translit:"msḏr",gloss:"In transliteration: the ear, carved in pairs and in multiples on votive stelae as the god's point of reception."},{native:"Bs",translit:"Bs (Bes)",gloss:"In transliteration: the dwarf god of the household and of childbirth, in whose painted chambers at Saqqara petitioners are thought to have slept for dreams."}],texts:[{title:"Papyrus Chester Beatty III (BM EA 10683)",note:"Ramesside dream manual on the recto, from the Deir el-Medina library of Qenherkhepeshef: entries classified by the type of the dreamer, verdicts of good and bad written in black and red, and a closing spell against nightmare."},{title:"The Oracular Amuletic Decrees (I. E. S. Edwards, Hieratic Papyri in the British Museum, Fourth Series: Oracular Amuletic Decrees of the Late New Kingdom)",note:"Third Intermediate Period rolls worn in cylindrical cases, in which Amun or Khonsu promises in the first person to protect a named child against an itemised catalogue of dangers."},{title:"The oracle ostraca of Deir el-Medina",note:"Records of questions put to the deified Amenhotep I as his barque was carried in procession, settling thefts, property disputes and appointments; the barque moved forward for yes and back for no."},{title:"The Dream Stela of Thutmose IV, Giza",note:"Set between the paws of the Great Sphinx: the prince sleeps in the shadow of the head and the god promises him the kingship in exchange for clearing the sand — a dream deployed as legitimation."},{title:"The archive of Hor of Sebennytos, Saqqara",note:"Second-century BC Demotic ostraca, edited by J. D. Ray, in which a temple functionary records his dreams and the official reports he made of them."},{title:"The Serapeum papers of Ptolemaios son of Glaukias",note:"Greek documents from Memphis of the second century BC: the dream journal and petitions of a recluse living in confinement within the sacred precinct."}]}]};export{e as default};
